.TH VARCO 1 "2026-11-03" "varco 1.0.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
varco \- command line interface of the Varco firewall
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B varco
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B varco
talks to the
.BR varcod (8)
service over the D-Bus system bus. Every operation is authorized by polkit:
viewing and deciding about connections is allowed to the user of the active
desktop session, changing protection, rules or settings requires the
administrator password. Run it with
.B sudo
from a terminal without a graphical polkit agent.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show whether protection is active, paused or isolated (kill switch).
.TP
.B monitor
Interactive view: shows new connections waiting for a decision and lets you
allow or block them with one letter
.RB ( s ", " w ", " p ", " t ", " u ", " n ", " d ", " k ", " x ).
.B w
allows always, but only on the same kind of network (Wi-Fi, mobile, cable, VPN).
.TP
.BR rules " [" \-\-json ]
List permanent rules.
.TP
.BR connections " [" \-\-json ]
List active connections and services listening on the network, with the
program that owns them.
.TP
.B add \fIallow\fR|\fIdeny\fR \fIout\fR|\fIin\fR|\fI*\fR \fIprogram\fR \fIhost\fR \fIport\fR [\fItcp\fR|\fIudp\fR|\fI*\fR] [\fB\-\-network\fR \fInet\fR]
Add a rule.
.I net
is \fB*\fR (default), \fB@wifi\fR, \fB@mobile\fR, \fB@ethernet\fR, \fB@vpn\fR or an
interface name pattern.
.I host
may be an address, a network in CIDR notation, a name with shell-style
wildcards (for example \fI*.debian.org\fR), \fB@dns\fR (the DNS servers this
computer is using now, which change with the network) or \fB*\fR.
A rule for an unknown program (\fB?\fR) that allows traffic must name one host
and one port.
.TP
.B remove \fIid\fR
Remove a rule.
.TP
.B killswitch on\fR|\fBoff
Isolate the computer from every network immediately, or reopen it.
.TP
.B pause on\fR|\fBoff
Pause protection (the network is free and not checked) or resume it.
.TP
.B set \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Change a setting: \fIdecision_timeout\fR, \fIkillswitch_auto\fR (off, in, in_out),
\fIwhois\fR, \fIwatchdog\fR, \fIlog_allowed\fR, \fImax_pending\fR.
\fIwhois\fR and \fIlog_allowed\fR are off by default: whois sends every new
address to the internet registries, and \fIlog_allowed\fR keeps in the journal
every site the computer connects to.
.TP
.B kill \fIpid\fR
Terminate a process.
.TP
.B reload
Reload configuration and rules.
.TP
.B log
Follow the service log with
.BR journalctl (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if the service is not running or the operation is not authorized.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR varco-gtk (1),
.BR varcod (8),
.BR varco-privacy (8)
